Objective
To learn how to securely configure an Amazon S3 bucket and protect stored objects from unauthorized or public access.

What I Practiced
1. Created an S3 Bucket
Created the bucket:
taiwo-cloud-security-practical-6
The bucket was configured with security-focused settings.

2. Blocked Public Access
Enabled Block all public access.
This prevents the bucket and its objects from being publicly accessible through common S3 public-access configurations.

3. Object Ownership
Configured Object Ownership with ACLs disabled.
This allows the bucket owner to control ownership without relying on traditional S3 Access Control Lists.

4. Bucket Versioning
Kept Versioning disabled for this practical.
Versioning allows S3 to retain previous versions of objects when they are changed or deleted.

5. Default Encryption
Configured Server-side encryption with Amazon S3 managed keys (SSE-S3).
This means S3 automatically encrypts objects stored in the bucket.

6. Uploaded an Object
Uploaded a test file named:
techStore
The upload was successful.

7. Checked Object Permissions
Inspected the Access Control List (ACL) for the techStore object and confirmed that public access was not being granted.
Security Concepts Learned
Amazon S3 buckets and objects
S3 public-access protection
Object Ownership
S3 ACLs
Bucket Versioning
Server-side encryption
SSE-S3
Basic S3 access control

